{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeEhz6aZCergyjMZxp73Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015ABd5BGogVacVFdxWSdhoc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/patrickd/deepseek-v4-flash-0731-cuda/evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_1b2.txt","content":"1b.2: the KV cache packs 2048 -> 720 B per row bit-exactly, and it buys the context term at the flat term's expense -- +3.233 +/- 0.203 ms/forward against -0.4996 +/- 0.0290 per 1000 ctx, break-even at ctx 6,471\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. include/kv_pack.h and DSV4_KV_PACK=1. The main KV cache already stored values that sit\nexactly on the E4M3 grid with an exact power-of-two scale, and then kept them in FP32 -- 8 bits of\ninformation in 32 bits per element. Now it stores what was computed: 448 E4M3 bytes + 7 UE8M0 scale\nbytes + the 64 RoPE dims still FP32 = 711 B of payload in a 720 B stride, 2.844x. 720 is the\nsmallest 16 B-aligned stride, which is what lets sparse_attn gather a row with vector loads, and it\nis divisible by 4, so a cache row stays `base + row * g_kv_rowf` FLOATS in both layouts -- the one\ndecision that kept this to a stride substitution at ~30 call sites instead of a retyping of every\ncache pointer in the engine. The producer seam is kv_stage/kv_commit, and in the FP32 arm kv_stage\nreturns the cache row itself while kv_commit IS the act_quant_fp8sim call that was already there:\nthe before-arm is unchanged CODE, not merely unchanged behaviour.\n\nBIT-EXACT, PROVEN FOUR WAYS AND THEN BY THE ENGINE. Packing keeps the e4m3 byte and the exponent\nbyte; unpacking replays the same two conversions and the same multiply the write path performed. So\nthe acceptance test is memcmp, and required n is zero at a flip rate of zero.\n  * gate_kv_pack -- round trip vs act_quant_fp8sim on 524,288 floats x 5 distributions x {raw,\n    re-quantised}, 0 mismatches; reader vs FP32 reader at 11 (hpb,smem) launches x 5 engine shapes,\n    0 differing bytes. Two negative controls (code byte, scale byte) both fail as they must.\n  * gate_kv_pack_e2e -- the WIRING, which the first gate says nothing about: the real\n    compressed_attn_cache[_r4] / decode_step_{strided,indexer} / verify_step_* functions, once per\n    layout, 0 of 114,688 floats differ, and again with --swap on the arm order.\n  * gate_compressed_graph / gate_indexer_graph now call kv_pack_init() and PRINT the layout, which\n    is what turns them into a real test of kv_store_at/kv_store_comp -- the device-pos store kernels\n    nothing else exercises. PASS, maxabs 0.00e+00, both layouts.\n  * Engine: identical generated ids on both build/decode arms, tau 2.87 on all six replicates,\n    LOSSLESS x3 on both, and 16 of 16 server legs byte-identical with tau and mean verify width\n    equal on every leg.\n\nTHE BAND, both arms in one session on one build, control arm first so drift flatters the OLD layout:\n  ctx      before -> after   paired   tok/s\n   1,656   130.27  132.45    +2.18    14.27 -> 14.04\n   3,197   132.77  134.74    +1.97    12.44 -> 12.26\n   6,248   137.07  137.18    +0.10    12.23 -> 12.22\n   6,260   134.16  134.17    +0.01    12.13 -> 12.12\n  12,410   142.27  139.31    -2.97    12.09 -> 12.34  (+2.1 %)\nRegressed on context: +3.233 +/- 0.203 flat, -0.4996 +/- 0.0290 per 1000, R^2 0.990, both 2 SE bands\nexcluding zero. The independent per-arm fits with the width term controlled give b 0.924 -> 0.381\nand a 82.83 -> 85.74 -- the same answer by a different route. So b 1.887 -> 1.387 (b x 6592\n12.44 -> 9.15, -26 %) and a 125.11 -> 128.34 (+2.6 %). Break-even ctx 6,471.\n\nWHY A BYTE REDUCTION MOVED b AND COST a. KV_PRECISION_FINDINGS section 4 said in advance this is not\na bandwidth-bound path, and half of that was right. The FLAT cost is instructions: sparse_attn\nunpacks every gathered row and 1.7 established that kernel is issue-bound. gate_kv_pack --bench\nprices it without a checkpoint -- the packed reader is 0.776x / 0.805x / 0.866x / 0.820x / 0.904x of\nthe FP32 reader at the five engine shapes. A third fewer bytes, and the kernel got SLOWER at every\none of them. The CONTEXT saving is bytes: the gathered set is topk rows and topk grows with context,\nso packing shrinks exactly the part that scales -- that section's own L2-residency hypothesis,\nlabelled \"hypothesis, not measurement\", now measured and landing on the term it named.\n\nTHE FIRST IMPLEMENTATION WAS 55 % WORSE ON TERM A, AND THE FIX INVERTS AN INSTINCT. The staging loop\nread a uint4 -- 16 codes per thread, only 28 vector loads for 448 codes, the better number by every\nroofline metric. The block is 128 threads at hpb=4, so 28 threads did sixteen unpacks each while 100\nwaited, on the double-buffered prefetch's critical path: 0.699x on the bench, +4.998 +/- 0.374\nms/forward in the engine (that arm is kept at fit_1b2_*_v1*). Four codes per thread -- FOUR TIMES\nthe load instructions -- took it to +3.233 +/- 0.203 while leaving the slope alone\n(-0.4653 +/- 0.0534 -> -0.4996 +/- 0.0290, overlapping). A clean separation: the fix touched issue,\nnot bytes. The (hpb,smem) retune was then checked rather than assumed and is a NULL -- the packed\noptimum is the same hpb=4/smem=2 launch 1.7 chose for FP32.\n\nDEFAULT OFF. a is 88 % of the forward and the ladder's binding term; this makes a worse to make b\nbetter, and the two cancel almost exactly at the ladder's own 6592 reference (net -0.06 ms). Where\nthat trade lands depends on the operating context, so it is written down rather than decided\nsilently: above ctx ~6.5k DSV4_KV_PACK=1 is a throughput win, below it a loss.\n\nAND THE ITEM'S OWN JUSTIFICATION WAS WRONG, SO IT IS CORRECTED RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPED.\n\"Unlocks seqmax 32k-64k, which does not fit today\" came from KV_PRECISION_FINDINGS section 3, which\npriced the KV cache in isolation and never checked the resident set. Both layouts load at seqmax\n32768 and serve a completion -- FP32 mem 115.3/122.8 GiB, packed 115.1. What packing buys is\nheadroom and 65536, not 32768. And that mem line does NOT show the 2.08 GiB the arithmetic predicts\nbetween those arms, because cudaMalloc on this unified pool costs no physical page until it is\nTOUCHED: a capacity claim taken at load time measures the allocator, not the machine. The saving is\nclaimed from the allocation arithmetic instead -- 2.844x on every KV allocation, 1.61 -> 0.57 GiB at\nseqmax 16384, 3.21 -> 1.13 at 32768, 6.43 -> 2.26 at 65536.\n\nONE GAP, STATED. mla_decode_step_dp -- the sliding-layer device-pos path, 2 of 43 layers, reachable\nonly through build/decode GRAPH=1 -- is wired but has no gate in EITHER layout, because none existed\nbefore this item either. The compressed device-pos paths, the other 41 layers, are covered.\n\nWiki: kernel-optimisations.md section 3, negative-results.md 4i, context-scaling.md,\nmeasurement-and-traps.md 30 and 31, KV_PRECISION_FINDINGS.md section 3 correction, README state\ntable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6182,"cache_read_input_tokens":405184,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6182},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"465b48d7-53a2-44ff-a690-c468523649f2","uuid":"38287e52-b73d-41d5-8963-dd727fd325b7","timestamp":"2026-08-20T21:26:43.151Z","request_id":"req_011CeEhz4Y2PqBipMqri5Pwe"}
